  </category>

  <!-- ── Tier 2: Symbols ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE 8 UNDOCUMENTED SYMBOLS</pattern>
    <template>Tier 2 of the Brass Note Method involves eight symbols that control vocal performance at the micro level — things like pauses, breath, whisper delivery, and syllabic rendering that standard Suno prompting cannot touch. Two of the symbols are public: "..." (pause/rest in delivery) and "(( ))" (hushed, intimate vocal treatment). The remaining six are proprietary and taught exclusively inside Brass Syntax Academy.
      <nav-card label="The Lyrical Syntax" href="/method#tier2" description="Eight symbols. Eight repeatable results."/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE UNDOCUMENTED SYMBOLS</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT ARE THE 8 UNDOCUMENTED SYMBOLS</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE LYRICAL SYNTAX</pattern>
    <template>The Lyrical Syntax is Tier 2 of the Brass Note Method — a set of eight symbols discovered through systematic stress testing of Suno v5.5's lyrical processing engine. Each symbol controls a specific element of vocal performance: pauses, breath, emotional color, syllabic rendering, and more. Two symbols are public; six are proprietary to Brass Syntax Academy.
      <nav-card label="Tier 2: Lyrical Syntax" href="/method#tier2"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS TIER TWO OF THE METHOD</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT IS THE LYRICAL SYNTAX</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS TIER 2 OF THE METHOD</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT IS THE LYRICAL SYNTAX</srai></template>
  </category>

  <!-- ── Tier 3: Neural Bypass ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE NEURAL BYPASS</pattern>
    <template>Neural Bypass is Tier 3 of the Brass Note Method — a technique for bypassing Suno's instrumental training associations by replacing instrument names with phonetic descriptions of what those instruments sound like. When the model's only pathway is vocal, it produces vocal. This is how we achieve full vocal symphonies, eight-part a cappella arrangements with zero instrument bleed, and beatbox percussion with audible breath — consistently, through prompting alone.
      <nav-card label="Tier 3: Neural Bypass" href="/method#tier3"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS PHONETIC INSTRUMENT DESCRIPTION</pattern>
    <template>Phonetic Instrument Description is the core technique of Tier 3 — replacing instrument names with acoustic descriptions of what those instruments sound like. When a prompt contains an instrument name, the model's training routes directly to that instrument regardless of what else the prompt says. Removing the trigger word and describing the sound instead forces the model to render the role vocally. It requires actual musicianship — knowing what instruments do, the space they occupy, the physical sound they produce.
      <nav-card label="Tier 3: Neural Bypass" href="/method#tier3"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS TIER THREE OF THE METHOD</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT IS THE NEURAL BYPASS</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS TIER 3 OF THE METHOD</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT IS THE NEURAL BYPASS</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO YOU GET VOCALS ONLY</pattern>
    <template>Getting purely vocal output from Suno is one of the most documented challenges in the AI music community — the standard approach of writing "vocals only" or "no instruments" reliably fails. Tier 3 of the Brass Note Method addresses this by replacing instrument names with phonetic descriptions of what those instruments sound like, removing the trigger words the model routes to. The result is vocal output that's consistent across nearly every generation.
      <nav-card label="The Neural Bypass Technique" href="/method#tier3"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>CAN YOU DO VOCALS ONLY TRACKS</pattern>
    <template><srai>HOW DO YOU GET VOCALS ONLY</srai></template>
  </category>

  <!-- ── Case Studies ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE CASE STUDIES</pattern>
    <template>The Brass Note Method page documents two case studies — both producing results the Suno AI community considers unreliable or impossible through standard prompting. Case Study 01 is "The A Cappella Breakthrough": an eight-part a cappella arrangement with zero instrument bleed, produced through prompting alone on Suno v5.5. Case Study 02 is "The Vocal Symphony": eleven distinct instrument roles — bass, guitar, percussion, and more — all rendered entirely by human voices.
      <nav-card label="See the Case Studies" href="/method#casestudies"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>TELL ME ABOUT THE CASE STUDIES</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT ARE THE CASE STUDIES</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ACAPPELLA BREAKTHROUGH</pattern>
    <template>"The A Cappella Breakthrough" is Case Study 01 on our Method page. Using the Brass Note Method on Suno v5.5, we produced a complete eight-part a cappella arrangement — male tenor lead, female quartet, vocal bass, vocal percussion, mouth clicks — with zero instrument bleed. No external tools, no post-processing, no stem separation. Clean through prompting alone, consistently across multiple generations. The Suno community's own documentation confirms this is considered unreliable through standard approaches.
      <nav-card label="See Case Study 01" href="/method#casestudies"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE A CAPPELLA BREAKTHROUGH</pattern>
    <template><srai>WHAT IS THE ACAPPELLA BREAKTHROUGH</srai></template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>CAN YOU DO ACAPPELLA</pattern>
    <template>Yes — and with zero instrument bleed, which the Suno community considers very difficult to achieve reliably. Our Method page documents how we produced an eight-part a cappella arrangement through prompting alone on Suno v5.5 — no post-processing, no stem separation tools. It's one of the documented results of Tier 3 of the Brass Note Method.
      <nav-card label="The A Cappella Case Study" href="/method#casestudies"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE VOCAL SYMPHONY</pattern>
    <template>"The Vocal Symphony" is Case Study 02 on our Method page. Using Tier 3 of the Brass Note Method, we produced a complete vocal symphony where eleven distinct instrument roles — bass, rhythm guitar, lead guitar, crash cymbal, snare, hi-hat, wah effects — were each rendered entirely by human voices. Audible breath and lip sounds throughout confirm physically human performance rather than synthesized approximation. Consistent output across nearly every generation on Suno v5.5.
      <nav-card label="See Case Study 02" href="/method#casestudies"/>
    </template>
  </category>

  <!-- ── Brass Syntax Academy / Masterclass ────────────────────────────── -->

  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS BRASS SYNTAX ACADEMY</pattern>
    <template>Brass Syntax Academy is the Brass Note Method masterclass — where the full framework is taught in detail. It covers the specific parameters for all ten layers, the complete eight-symbol lyrical syntax reference, and the phonetic instrument description library used in Tier 3. There are three learning paths: Songwriting Track, Production Track, and the Full Method Track. The Academy is not yet open — you can join the waitlist on our Method page.
      <nav-card label="Join the Waitlist" href="/method#waitlist" description="Founding member pricing when enrollment opens"/>
    </template>
  </category>

